## Founding & History
Holm Oak Vineyards was originally planted in 1983 by Justice Edward Butler and his son Nicholas, making it one of Tasmania's older vineyards. The first wines were produced by the Butlers in 1987, and Riesling vines were added in 1991. The property was purchased in 2004 by Robyn and Ian Wilson, the parents of current winemaker Bec Duffy. Bec Duffy and her husband Tim Duffy then bought the property from her parents in 2013. The name "Holm Oak" refers to the property's original use in the 1930s for growing Holm Oak trees, intended for making tennis racquets for the Alexander Patent Racquet Co.. However, the oak wood did not meet the required standards for racquet production. Since 2006, Bec and Tim Duffy have been dedicated to crafting cool-climate Tasmanian wines.

## People
Holm Oak Vineyards is a family-owned operation run by husband and wife team Bec Duffy and Tim Duffy. Bec Duffy is the winemaker, with over 20 years of experience in Australia and the US. She returned to Tasmania in 2006 to take on Holm Oak. Tim Duffy is the viticulturist, a third-generation grape grower and agronomist. Their complementary skills contribute to their vision of producing wines that reflect the Tamar Valley and their personalities. The winery also has resident dogs, Nyah and Bella, who greet visitors at the cellar door. Additionally, two pigs, Pinot Junior (self-appointed General Manager) and Mayonnaise, are notable residents that visitors can interact with.

## Vineyard & Site
Holm Oak has 18 hectares under vine. Another source states 14 hectares, and another 12 hectares. The vineyards are located on the west slopes of the Tamar River, facing east to catch the morning sun. The property slopes towards the river. The majority of the soil is acidic Grey Kandosol on Permian mudstone. Some areas of the property are too rocky for viticultural production, requiring blasting to penetrate the sheet rock. The region is known for aromatic whites and Pinot Noir.

Varietals planted include:
* Riesling: 0.8 hectares, established in 1991.
* Pinot Gris: 2.5 hectares, established in 2007, 2013, and 2023. Another source states 2.5 hectares established in 2007 and 2013.
* Chardonnay: 2.5 hectares, made up of 4 clones, established in 2007, 2008, 2014, and 2023. Another source states 2 hectares established in 2007, 2008, and 2017.
* Pinot Noir: 9 hectares. Clones include MV6, D5V12, 114, 115, 777, D4V2, 521, Abel, and 777. The MV6 and D5V12 clones were established in 1983, making them some of Tasmania's oldest Pinot Noir vines.
* Cabernet Sauvignon: 1.8 hectares, established in 1983.
* Merlot: 0.6 hectares, established in 2013 and 2020.
* Cabernet Franc: 0.3 hectares, established in 1983 and 2020.
* Arneis: 0.3 hectares, established in 2007. Another source states 0.3 hectares established in 2009.
* Shiraz: 0.2 hectares, established in 2015.

Vine spacing varies from 2000 to 4000 vines per hectare, with plantings from 2007 onwards being just over 2000 vines per hectare. All plantings run North/South to maximize sun exposure.

Regarding farming approach, Holm Oak uses many organic products in the vineyard but is not certified organic due to the Tamar Valley's climate and high pressure from fungal diseases. They aim for minimal intervention and use pesticides that are organic. Herbicides are minimized and applied only as needed. They are part of Wine Tasmania's VinØ sustainability program, which focuses on soil health, responsible pest and disease control, biodiversity, water usage, waste management, and personnel welfare. Most harvesting and pruning are carried out by hand. The majority of Pinot Noir is hand-harvested, allowing for identification and processing of nuances in different fruit parcels. Fruit sorting is done in the vineyard, with inferior or diseased bunches removed prior to picking. Irrigation is used sparingly, as annual rainfall in the Rowella area is around 600-660mm.

## Cellar Door Experience
The Holm Oak Vineyards cellar door is located in the Tamar Valley, approximately a 40-minute drive from Launceston. Bookings are recommended for all tasting experiences, especially for groups. The cellar door is open Monday to Friday from 10 am to 4 pm for seated wine flights. During April to October, hours are 10 am to 3 pm Monday to Friday. It is closed from December 25th-27th and on Good Friday.

Visitors can choose from two main wine flight tasting experiences: the 'Choose Your Own Adventure Wine Flight', which allows selection of four wines from a choice of nine, and a Pinot Noir wine flight, showcasing five different styles of Pinot Noir. There is also a "Top of our Game Tasting Experience" which is tennis-inspired and features "Wizard" and "Hot Shot" wines, accompanied by tennis memorabilia. A "Sensory Garden Experience" is also available, with self-guided tours.

The cellar door offers a relaxed setting to sample wines. Guests can enjoy tastings by the fire in winter or with scenic vineyard views in summer. Visitors can also create their own platter or picnic with local cheeses and small goods to enjoy overlooking the vineyards. The cellar door is family-friendly, and dogs are welcome with prior notification. Visitors can feed the resident pigs, Pinot Junior and Mayonnaise, and meet the winery dogs, Nyah and Bella.

## Food & Hospitality
Holm Oak Vineyards does not operate a full restaurant or kitchen. However, visitors can choose a bottle of wine and select from a range of local cheeses and small goods available at the cellar door to create their own platter or picnic. These can be enjoyed overlooking the vineyards.

## Awards & Recognition
Holm Oak Vineyards has received a James Halliday Five Star Winery rating. Their cellar door won Gold in the Wineries category at the 2025 Tasmanian Tourism Awards.

## Sustainability & Certifications
Holm Oak Vineyards uses many organic products in their vineyard but is not certified organic due to the climate in the Tamar Valley and the high pressure of fungal diseases. They strive for minimal intervention and use organic pesticides. Herbicides are minimized and used only as needed. They are part of Wine Tasmania's VinØ sustainability program, an industry-wide initiative focused on soil health, responsible pest and disease control, biodiversity, water usage, waste management, and looking after vineyard personnel. Winemaker Bec Duffy notes that becoming fully organic is not considered achievable in their situation, but they continue to be as minimal intervention as possible.

## Visiting Practical Info
* **Address**: 11 West Bay Road (Highway C724), Rowella, Tasmania, 7270.
* **Distance from major town**: Approximately a 40-minute drive from Launceston.
* **Phone**: (03) 6394 7577. Mobile numbers are 0455 027 899 (cellar door enquiries) and 0439 408 933 (general enquiries).
* **Website**: http://www.holmoakvineyards.com.au/.
* **Email**: cellardoor@holmoakvineyards.com.au for cellar door enquiries, and admin@holmoakvineyards.com.au for general enquiries.
* **Social Media**: Links to Facebook and Instagram are available on some listings.
* **Accessibility**: Disabled access is available; contact the operator for details.
* **Other**: Electric Vehicle Charging Point available. The vineyard participates in the Tasmanian Mixed Dozen freight scheme.

## Notable / Distinctive
Holm Oak Vineyards has a unique history tied to the Alexander Patent Racquet Company, which originally planted Holm Oak trees on the site in the 1930s for tennis racquet production. This heritage is reflected in their "The Wizard" Pinot Noir, named after a famous tennis racquet used by Jack Crawford when he won Wimbledon in 1933. The cellar door offers a tennis-inspired tasting experience with memorabilia.

The winery is known for its resident animals, including two friendly winery dogs, Nyah and Bella, and two pigs, Pinot Junior (self-appointed General Manager) and Mayonnaise, which visitors can feed.

Holm Oak features Tasmania's only sensory wine garden, which visitors can explore through self-guided tours. This garden is designed to embody the aroma, taste, and texture of the distinctive wine varietals produced.

Winemaker Bec Duffy and viticulturist Tim Duffy are a husband-and-wife team, with Bec having been born on King Island, Tasmania, and inspired to pursue winemaking after her father planted trial vines. They are known for their hands-on approach and commitment to producing single vineyard wines with personality and character.

